# Shinobi — Current State

Updated: 2026-08-27

## Status

The Shinobi repository has a green baseline on `main`. The current implementation already contains the beginnings of application resources, application resolution, filesystem application storage, deployment loading, and binding resolution.

This branch, `feat/shinobi-node-mvp`, is the implementation branch for turning that foundation into a runnable Shinobi node.

## Locked direction

Shinobi is a node runtime built on Codejitsu.

```text
Codejitsu
   |
   v
Shinobi Node
   |
   +-- Config Scrolls -> listeners/routes/bindings
   |
   +-- App Scrolls -> composable applications
   |
   +-- Swoole -> node runtime/listener
   |
   +-- Codejitsu CLI -> Codex -> Command Scrolls
```

App Scrolls describe applications. Config Scrolls describe deployment. These concerns remain separate.

The MVP must support a Config Scroll mapping multiple hosts to one App Scroll, for example:

```text
shinobiforge.local       -> app://forge#1.0
auth.shinobiforge.local  -> app://forge#1.0
```

App Scrolls must be composable/inheritable with deterministic precedence and cycle rejection.

## CLI direction

Codejitsu owns the reusable CLI runtime. Symfony Console is the initial driver behind a swappable driver boundary. Commands are discovered through the Codex as Command Scrolls.

A Codejitsu project created with Composer gets `bin/$project_name`, `scrolls/`, and generator stubs. Stub resolution is:

```text
CLI --stub override
  -> project stub
  -> package stub
  -> Codejitsu default
```

Shinobi contributes node/application/generator Command Scrolls rather than creating a second CLI architecture.

Initial Shinobi commands:

```text
node:start
node:stop
node:restart
node:reload
node:info
nodes
apps:list
apps:add
apps:remove
apps:info
make:app
make:config
```

## Runtime direction

The first runnable node uses OpenSwoole directly. The first acceptance path is local HTTP:

```text
HTTP request
  -> Swoole listener
  -> route/binding resolver
  -> App Scroll
  -> Codejitsu execution
  -> response
```

Nginx, Caddy, Traefik, or another edge provider is optional future infrastructure for TLS termination, authentication, WAF/rate limits, tenant routing, or container isolation. Edge infrastructure must not become an App Scroll dependency.

## Ecosystem direction

The intended application progression is compositional:

```text
Shinobi
  -> ArchIQ and/or Kage
     -> Sensei
```

ArchIQ and Kage should remain independently useful. Sensei may compose capabilities from both.

## Next slice

1. Complete Codejitsu CLI driver/Codex command registration.
2. Complete Codejitsu project skeleton and reusable generator/stub resolution.
3. Make Shinobi App Scroll loading/composition deterministic.
4. Normalize deployment into listeners and routes and extend binding resolution for multiple hosts/ports.
5. Implement the Shinobi node lifecycle and Swoole HTTP request path.
6. Register Shinobi node/application/generator commands as Command Scrolls.
7. Add canonical `shinobi.app` and derived application fixtures and prove the full request path.
8. Verify clean install, CLI discovery, node lifecycle, and host-based application routing.

## Guardrails

- Preserve existing green tests.
- Use existing Codejitsu discovery/Codex/resource boundaries instead of parallel registries.
- Keep external runtimes behind narrow adapters.
- Keep deployment infrastructure out of App Scrolls.
- Prefer small, behaviorally tested vertical slices.
- Update `.context` whenever architecture or durable behavior changes.
